1
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.439
[S01] Thank you all for joining us.

2
00:00:10.439 --> 00:00:12.240
[S01] I'd like to introduce you to Ray Dalio.

3
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.449
[S01] He's the founder of Bridgewater Associates.

4
00:00:14.449 --> 00:00:19.890
[S01] He founded it in 1975 and is one of the most successful hedge funds in history.

5
00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.730
[S01] So let's give Ray and Ice Cube a welcome.

6
00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:29.109
[S02] Thank you for being here, Ray.

7
00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:34.539
[S02] I'm at a stage in my life, well, I'm entering what I call the third stage of my

8
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:36.539
[S02] life.

9
00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:40.140
[S02] I think of life as existing in three big stages.

10
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:48.619
[S02] The first is that you're learning from others, you're dependent on others, you're a kid.

11
00:00:48.619 --> 00:00:55.229
[S02] Second stage of your life is then you're working, others are dependent on you, and

12
00:00:55.229 --> 00:00:57.939
[S02] you're trying to be successful.

13
00:00:57.939 --> 00:01:03.850
[S02] Then, after you get to the later stage of life, third stage of your life, is others

14
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.310
[S02] are successful without you.

15
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:11.950
[S02] And that you're free, according to Joseph Campbell, free to live and free to die.

16
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:12.950
[S02] Okay?

17
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.609
[S02] So that you have that element of freedom.

18
00:01:14.609 --> 00:01:20.420
[S02] And so I'm at a stage in my life where, and I started Bridgewater out of a two-bedroom

19
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:31.209
[S02] apartment in 1975, and I've brought it to where it is now, according to Fortune, it's

20
00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:34.859
[S02] the fifth most important private company in the United States.

21
00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:40.819
[S02] It's been successful, it's been good, and that my objective at this particular stage

22
00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:43.109
[S02] is to help others be successful without me.

23
00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:48.310
[S02] I learned along the way certain principles.

24
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:55.359
[S02] Every time I would make a decision, I would write down the reasons I would make that decision.

25
00:01:55.359 --> 00:02:02.969
[S02] And I put them out, I debated them, and I developed these principles.

26
00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:06.310
[S02] So think about principles as just being these reasons for making a decision.

27
00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.879
[S02] If you're in this situation, how do you deal with it?

28
00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:17.689
[S02] It was also very important to me to operate in a very unusual way that seemed very sensible

29
00:02:17.689 --> 00:02:20.310
[S02] to me, and it's an idea meritocratic way.

30
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:24.719
[S02] So I want to describe Bridgewater as being an idea meritocracy.

31
00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:28.620
[S02] In other words, a real idea meritocracy, which I'll explain and show to you.

32
00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:35.409
[S02] So a real idea meritocracy, in which the goals are meaningful work and meaningful relationships.

33
00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:39.969
[S02] Meaningful work, I mean you're on a mission, that you feel you're on a mission together

34
00:02:39.969 --> 00:02:42.439
[S02] to do those great things.

35
00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:48.340
[S02] And meaningful relationships, meaning that you care about each other, it's part of a

36
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.560
[S02] community, and to be on that mission together.

37
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.009
[S02] And that was really great in terms of our success, but it was meaningful work and meaningful

38
00:02:56.009 --> 00:03:02.229
[S02] relationships through radical truthfulness and radical transparency.

39
00:03:02.229 --> 00:03:08.060
[S02] That means literally people saying anything that they feel that they want to say, in

40
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:13.310
[S02] terms of being polite, of course, but sharing what they really believe is true and working

41
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:20.310
[S02] themselves through to have an idea meritocratic way, and to literally record everything for

42
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.810
[S02] everybody to hear.

43
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:28.039
[S02] So I mean literally, there are a combination of videos and tapes of all meetings that

44
00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:31.370
[S02] happen, so that nothing is hidden.

45
00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.090
[S02] Because you can't have a real idea meritocracy if you can't see things yourself.

46
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:43.699
[S02] And so it's a very unusual place, and it was really the basis of our success.

47
00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:49.009
[S02] And I want to explain that way of operating to you, because this idea meritocratic way

48
00:03:49.009 --> 00:03:54.009
[S02] in which there's meaningful work and meaningful relationships through radical truthfulness

49
00:03:54.009 --> 00:04:00.229
[S02] and radical transparency, so that you could have thoughtful disagreement and have ways

50
00:04:00.229 --> 00:04:05.900
[S02] of getting past that disagreement to then move on, like a legal system, that has been

51
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.509
[S02] the key to our success.

52
00:04:07.509 --> 00:04:10.229
[S02] So that's what I want to try to convey to you.

53
00:04:10.229 --> 00:04:13.509
[S02] And I'm just going to take a few minutes to try to go through a few slides to give you

54
00:04:13.509 --> 00:04:14.759
[S02] a sense of this.

55
00:04:14.759 --> 00:04:20.410
[S02] You know, I wanted big audacious goals, I wish big audacious goals for you.

56
00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:25.829
[S02] Go after your goals, and on your way to your goals, you're going to encounter your problems

57
00:04:25.829 --> 00:04:27.139
[S02] and your failures.

58
00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:28.139
[S02] Right?

59
00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:29.139
[S02] That's going to happen.

60
00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:30.639
[S02] Otherwise, you just go straight to your goals.

61
00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:32.120
[S02] No, that's the learning process.

62
00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.329
[S02] You encounter your failures, failures is part of the learning process.

63
00:04:36.329 --> 00:04:44.560
[S02] From the failures, what I found was great is I started to think of failures as lessons.

64
00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:51.879
[S02] I started to think of them as puzzles, rather than develop emotional reactions to those

65
00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:53.060
[S02] failures.

66
00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.639
[S02] I started to think pain plus quality reflection.

67
00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:59.089
[S03] Would make, give me progress.

68
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:06.889
[S03] So I started to think of almost the failures like puzzles.

69
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:13.790
[S03] That if I could study the puzzles, the puzzles was, what would I do differently in the future?

70
00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:16.990
[S03] That wouldn't produce that problem again when it happened.

71
00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:21.990
[S03] So, and then I would reflect, well, what was the, that, that would be my principles.

72
00:05:22.019 --> 00:05:23.990
[S03] What would I do differently in the future?

73
00:05:24.019 --> 00:05:26.990
[S03] If I solved that puzzle, I would get a gem.

74
00:05:27.019 --> 00:05:33.290
[S03] And the gem was principle, a principle to handle it better in the future.

75
00:05:33.319 --> 00:05:35.589
[S03] Cause failure is a learning process.

76
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.589
[S03] It's an essential part of the learning process.

77
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.990
[S03] If you can realize that and you write down those principles, write them down, it's been fantastic.

78
00:05:43.019 --> 00:05:46.790
[S03] So we learn those principles and then it would help me improve.

79
00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:49.389
[S03] And then I would go on to more audacious goals.

80
00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:55.389
[S03] And I look at evolution, personal evolution, or almost every evolution, evolution of a company,

81
00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:59.290
[S03] evolution of everything, as being this constant looping process.

82
00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:03.990
[S03] That I sort of think of as this five-step process, right?

83
00:06:04.019 --> 00:06:07.990
[S03] If, in other words, to be successful, you have to, you have to do five things.

84
00:06:08.019 --> 00:06:13.279
[S03] You have to, first, you have to know what your goals are and go after those goals.

85
00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:14.889
[S03] Be clear on your goals.

86
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.790
[S03] And you will encounter problems on the way to those goals.

87
00:06:17.819 --> 00:06:20.189
[S03] Those will be your barriers, okay?

88
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.689
[S03] They're your tests now.

89
00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.689
[S03] Don't just emotionally complain.

90
00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.689
[S03] Think about them as your problems.

91
00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.089
[S03] You have to diagnose those problems to the root cause, to get at the root cause.

92
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.490
[S03] And that root cause might be yourself, what you're doing wrong, or what somebody else is doing wrong.

93
00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:38.589
[S03] So you can't depersonalize it.

94
00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.490
[S03] You have to really look at it so that you make those changes.

95
00:06:42.519 --> 00:06:46.790
[S03] And when you get at that root cause, only by knowing that root cause,

96
00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:50.389
[S03] can then you design a way to get around that root cause.

97
00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:55.689
[S03] Like if you're not good at something yourself, it's okay if you find somebody else who's good

98
00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.990
[S03] at the things that you're not good at, cause nobody can be good at everything, right?

99
00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.790
[S03] So, but you have to do that.

100
00:07:01.819 --> 00:07:04.790
[S03] You just can't keep banging yourself in the wall.

101
00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:08.889
[S03] So you have to design something that's practical to get around with it.

102
00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.089
[S03] And then you have to follow through and do it.

103
00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:16.189
[S03] A lot of people come up with designs, but you have to do the thing that's necessary.

104
00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:20.990
[S03] And by trying that thing that's necessary, again, you will find out.

105
00:07:21.019 --> 00:07:25.589
[S03] Are you getting to your goals or you'll cop, or you encounter your next set of problems and so on?

106
00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.089
[S03] And that, I believe, is the first personal evolutionary process that has helped me.

107
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:35.990
[S03] Those rules that I was able to write down and that you can get in these, this book,

108
00:07:36.019 --> 00:07:40.089
[S03] Principles, which is why I'm passing them along, though that, those rules,

109
00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:46.689
[S03] we were actually able to then to put into algorithms and build decision-making processes

110
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:48.189
[S03] that replicate the brain.

111
00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.290
[S03] We'll get into that in a minute.

112
00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:58.089
[S03] Okay, so in order, in order to be successful in the markets, one has to be an independent thinker.

113
00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:03.089
[S03] In order to be an entrepreneur, one has to be an independent thinker.

114
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.389
[S03] And bet against the consensus and be right.

115
00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.889
[S03] Because the consensus in the markets is built into the price.

116
00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.589
[S03] Whatever anybody thinks, that's built into the price.

117
00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.589
[S03] So you have to think, you have to bet against the consensus.

118
00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:20.790
[S03] And you're gonna be wrong a fair amount of times about betting against the consensus.

119
00:08:20.819 --> 00:08:23.790
[S03] But in any case, you need to be an independent thinker.

120
00:08:23.819 --> 00:08:27.490
[S03] And for an entrepreneur, you need to be an independent thinker.

121
00:08:27.519 --> 00:08:32.490
[S03] Cause you're inventing new stuff, you're doing something, and you don't, don't know if you're right.

122
00:08:32.519 --> 00:08:37.889
[S03] So in order for me to be successful, I needed to have a bunch of independent thinkers

123
00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:41.090
[S03] in order to have them have, be effective.

124
00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.590
[S03] Okay, well, now, how are you gonna get this bunch of independent thinkers to agree on anything?

125
00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.090
[S03] Okay, I had to have an idea meritocracy.

126
00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:58.490
[S03] In other words, I had to have a system that literally systematically allows for thoughtful disagreement

127
00:08:58.519 --> 00:09:02.889
[S03] so that these idea meritocratic people could then get at the right answer.

128
00:09:02.919 --> 00:09:07.690
[S03] Because that's fantastic if you can have that thoughtful disagreement to get at the right answer.

129
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:08.889
[S03] It's very powerful.

130
00:09:08.919 --> 00:09:15.289
[S03] By having that and then systemizing it, principled systems, a system for having thoughtful disagreement,

131
00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:19.590
[S03] for an idea meritocracy, we could produce greater amounts of successes.

132
00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:24.789
[S03] We also produced failures, but we would look at that way, produce our learnings.

133
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:31.590
[S03] And that produced as a result, happy employees who really believe in this idea meritocratic thing

134
00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.690
[S03] and own the company intellectually and otherwise, own the company.

135
00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:37.389
[S03] And we had happy employees.

136
00:09:37.419 --> 00:09:40.990
[S03] And then it became easier to attract those types of people.

137
00:09:41.019 --> 00:09:45.690
[S03] Those types of people who believe that, you know, everybody has the right to make sense of things

138
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.190
[S03] and that there's a power in thoughtful disagreement.

139
00:09:48.519 --> 00:09:50.940
[S04] And that allowed us to attract more people.

140
00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:55.740
[S04] And that was the basis of going from the two-bedroom apartment to Bridgewater now.

141
00:09:55.769 --> 00:09:59.070
[S04] So we have about, now we have about fifteen hundred people.

142
00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.809
[S04] And that's, and that's how it works as an idea meritocracy.

143
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.639
[S04] And I want to pass that along to you.

144
00:10:05.669 --> 00:10:08.600
[S04] And I want to say, okay, so what is an idea meritocracy?

145
00:10:08.629 --> 00:10:10.470
[S04] You can have your own versions of this.

146
00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.210
[S04] You pick what is fine to you.

147
00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.879
[S04] But three different things are necessary for an idea meritocracy.

148
00:10:15.909 --> 00:10:22.250
[S04] First, you, everybody has to put their honest thoughts on the table to see.

149
00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:29.549
[S04] Now, I watch this, and I think so many organizations, so many people, they all keep it bottled up in their heads, right?

150
00:10:32.139 --> 00:10:33.340
[S04] You know?

151
00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.039
[S04] And they're critical behind the scenes.

152
00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:38.940
[S04] And that's bad.

153
00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:45.480
[S04] So can you put your honest thoughts on the table with other people's honest thoughts?

154
00:10:45.509 --> 00:10:49.009
[S04] So that you understand what people are thinking?

155
00:10:49.039 --> 00:10:49.509
[S04] Okay?

156
00:10:49.539 --> 00:10:52.600
[S04] It makes everything, first of all, a lot more efficient, right?

157
00:10:52.629 --> 00:10:56.490
[S04] It's terribly inefficient when everybody doesn't know what the other person's thinking.

158
00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:59.389
[S04] And then also, it doesn't allow ownership.

159
00:10:59.419 --> 00:11:03.990
[S04] It can't be an idea meritocracy if you just don't work it through.

160
00:11:04.029 --> 00:11:06.100
[S04] Everybody's talking behind the scenes.

161
00:11:06.129 --> 00:11:10.600
[S04] We don't allow talking behind the scenes, but anybody can challenge anything at any time.

162
00:11:10.629 --> 00:11:11.129
[S04] Okay?

163
00:11:11.159 --> 00:11:15.940
[S04] Then, you have to understand the art of thoughtful disagreement.

164
00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:17.539
[S04] Okay?

165
00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:19.070
[S04] Thoughtful disagreement.

166
00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.110
[S04] So many people react badly to disagreement.

167
00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:25.539
[S04] You have to change the modus operandi and start your thinking.

168
00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:27.509
[S04] It should be curiosity.

169
00:11:27.539 --> 00:11:29.009
[S04] How do you know who's right?

170
00:11:29.039 --> 00:11:30.710
[S04] How do you know who's wrong?

171
00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:31.269
[S04] Okay?

172
00:11:31.299 --> 00:11:34.279
[S04] It should prompt curiosity, not anger.

173
00:11:34.309 --> 00:11:39.610
[S04] It tends to produce some extent anger because it's like a barbaric animal behavior.

174
00:11:39.639 --> 00:11:44.070
[S04] That what happens is the amygdala part of us has this flight or flight thing.

175
00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.409
[S04] And then it's viewed as an attack, you know?

176
00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:49.610
[S04] And you have to, that's not good.

177
00:11:49.639 --> 00:11:54.970
[S04] So there are protocols that we have, that I won't go into now that I don't have time.

178
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:00.539
[S04] But they're laid out in the book of how do you have the art of thoughtful disagreement?

179
00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.409
[S04] To raise the probabilities.

180
00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:09.710
[S04] Because collectively, you're going, if you have an idea meritocracy, and you know how to do this well collectively,

181
00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:13.409
[S04] you're going to make much better decisions than individually.

182
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:19.139
[S04] If you just are stuck with the information that's in your head and your opinions, that's terrible.

183
00:12:19.169 --> 00:12:25.909
[S04] I believe that one of the greatest tragedies of mankind, such an easy thing to fix,

184
00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:32.110
[S04] is the people who are stuck with wrong opinions in their heads, that they don't put out there in stress tests

185
00:12:32.139 --> 00:12:35.279
[S04] and say, and raise their probabilities of being right.

186
00:12:35.309 --> 00:12:40.669
[S04] So the art of thoughtful disagreement, you have to understand the thoughtful disagreement

187
00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:47.509
[S04] that brings in better thinking than you individually has, and has a probability of moving you to a better answer

188
00:12:47.539 --> 00:12:49.470
[S04] than you would have individually.

189
00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.340
[S04] That requires a skill.

190
00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.970
[S04] And so then you have the disagreement.

191
00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:59.070
[S04] You know, that collective decision-making is very powerful if it's done well.

192
00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:02.940
[S04] But you might still have a disagreement after those things.

193
00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:03.570
[S04] You might come up.

194
00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.970
[S04] Then you have to have agreed-upon ways of getting past your disagreement.

195
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:14.669
[S04] If you have your disagreements that are gnawing at you and so on, it's like a law case, a legal case.

196
00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:21.169
[S04] Okay, you go in, you have a trial, you do it, you get, but you believe that the decision-making system is fair.

197
00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:26.909
[S04] And because you believe it's fair, and you've had that opportunity, that you can make your, that you say,

198
00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:30.970
[S04] now we can get past the disagreement and move on, rather than being stuck with it.

199
00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:35.440
[S04] So those are the three things that an idea meritocracy has to have, you know?

200
00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:40.279
[S04] And so if you think you want an idea meritocracy, you have to think about those three things.

201
00:13:40.309 --> 00:13:40.940
[S04] Okay?

202
00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.309
[S04] So this is the thing.

203
00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:52.409
[S04] Meaningful relationships and meaningful work together have produced this success because,

204
00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:58.470
[S04] and then this radical truthfulness and radical transparency, you know, produces an idea meritocracy.

205
00:13:58.500 --> 00:13:59.009
[S04] That's it.

206
00:13:59.039 --> 00:13:59.570
[S04] Okay?

207
00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:09.139
[S04] The other thing is, in order to have an idea meritocracy, and in order to have great personal development,

