00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:51.809
[S08] depending on the level of the dispute.

427
00:27:51.839 --> 00:27:55.730
[S08] Like, a very common thing is if you and I have a dispute,

428
00:27:55.759 --> 00:28:03.089
[S08] one of the simplest things to do is for you and I to agree, mutually agree on a mediator.

429
00:28:03.119 --> 00:28:04.369
[S09] Yep.

430
00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:06.009
[S08] So we agree on a mediator and move forward.

431
00:28:06.039 --> 00:28:11.970
[S08] Some others might be matters of principle and almost progresses like legal cases.

432
00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:12.730
[S08] What's your evidence?

433
00:28:12.759 --> 00:28:13.130
[S08] What's this?

434
00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:15.930
[S08] And who is the judge?

435
00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:19.130
[S09] Now, when you have this dialogue and discussion about feedback,

436
00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:23.450
[S09] and it might turn into a tense situation, like with disagreement,

437
00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:26.130
[S09] are there mediums of exchange that you prioritize?

438
00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:29.490
[S09] Because I, I see some people who will go towards email,

439
00:28:29.519 --> 00:28:33.849
[S09] but when you use email, you miss tone and content and context.

440
00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:38.849
[S08] As a generalization, we want the person to find the medium of exchange

441
00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:41.049
[S08] that they're most comfortable with.

442
00:28:41.079 --> 00:28:43.650
[S08] Because there are pros and cons to each.

443
00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:49.170
[S08] Like, some people in the moment feel that they're not as spontaneous in the moment,

444
00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.250
[S08] or they may be more introverted,

445
00:28:51.279 --> 00:28:53.930
[S08] and they think that they won't present themselves the best

446
00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:57.049
[S08] in a, in a person one-on-one discussion.

447
00:28:57.079 --> 00:29:01.970
[S08] So that they would prefer by email to say, listen, I'd like to lay out my thoughts,

448
00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:04.369
[S08] hear your thoughts by email,

449
00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.289
[S08] and then have that opportunity to have the back and forth.

450
00:29:07.319 --> 00:29:09.450
[S08] Some people would like to do it other ways.

451
00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:13.329
[S08] The important thing is that they just agree on what's most effective for them.

452
00:29:13.359 --> 00:29:14.170
[S09] Excellent.

453
00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:18.369
[S09] Now, let's say an organization goes through a calamity,

454
00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:22.250
[S09] and there's a trust issue in the organization.

455
00:29:22.279 --> 00:29:28.849
[S09] How does the company go about rebuilding the trust within employees?

456
00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:35.329
[S08] Well, again, I think radical truthfulness and radical transparency is the means of doing it.

457
00:29:35.359 --> 00:29:40.490
[S08] What, what I did, what my experience would be,

458
00:29:40.519 --> 00:29:44.440
[S08] if you have radical transparency,

459
00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:50.440
[S08] so literally every, every crisis I was in, every situation I was in,

460
00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:54.329
[S08] I would have a camera in that discussion

461
00:29:54.359 --> 00:29:57.490
[S08] as we looked at the pros and cons of the issue.

462
00:29:57.519 --> 00:30:01.250
[S08] So everybody could see that pros and, the pros and cons of the discussion.

463
00:30:01.279 --> 00:30:03.000
[S08] So you know there's no spin.

464
00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:08.339
[S10] You have a trust issue if it's hidden behind the scenes, I think.

465
00:30:08.369 --> 00:30:11.630
[S10] With that radical transparency, so everybody could see.

466
00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:16.490
[S10] Just imagine what it would be like here, okay?

467
00:30:16.519 --> 00:30:21.390
[S10] For whatever issue, that you could see how people are making decisions.

468
00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:25.700
[S10] Literally, weighing the pros and cons and seeing how they are real.

469
00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:29.990
[S10] And it could be anything from the top of the organization to just even somebody firing

470
00:30:30.019 --> 00:30:32.990
[S10] somebody else, so you don't, was it done well?

471
00:30:33.019 --> 00:30:39.119
[S10] That radical transparency, and then coming out of it and writing principles as to why

472
00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:41.289
[S10] did you handle it that way?

473
00:30:41.319 --> 00:30:45.880
[S10] And then putting those principles out so that everybody could debate that principle

474
00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:51.109
[S10] or look at that principle, not in enormous time-consuming way, but in effective way.

475
00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:55.539
[S10] And then having an idea meritocratic way where you look at that and you say, okay,

476
00:30:55.569 --> 00:31:00.480
[S10] that decision was made that way, maybe that's not the way I would do it, but I now understand

477
00:31:00.509 --> 00:31:02.450
[S10] the thinking behind it.

478
00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:08.700
[S10] Because if you don't have that kind of thing, then there's alienation.

479
00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:13.809
[S10] If you don't have trust and you don't have an appreciation, then increasingly you build

480
00:31:13.839 --> 00:31:17.029
[S10] an organization in which there's a we and them, you know?

481
00:31:17.059 --> 00:31:23.490
[S10] There's those people who are making the decisions and you don't really understand it and you

482
00:31:23.519 --> 00:31:25.819
[S10] just have to sort of go along with it.

483
00:31:25.849 --> 00:31:33.220
[S10] And then that becomes a source of tension and then there are factions and all that

484
00:31:33.250 --> 00:31:35.059
[S10] and there's no resolution.

485
00:31:35.089 --> 00:31:38.490
[S10] You have to have resolution and get past it.

486
00:31:38.519 --> 00:31:44.490
[S10] So, you know, the radical truthfulness and the radical transparency brings trust.

487
00:31:44.519 --> 00:31:50.000
[S10] It also means that bad stuff, it's tough for bad stuff to happen, because bad stuff

488
00:31:50.029 --> 00:31:51.079
[S10] happens in the dark.

489
00:31:52.309 --> 00:31:53.079
[S11] That's true.

490
00:31:53.109 --> 00:31:57.579
[S10] You make it radically transparent and everybody can bring it up, then everybody

491
00:31:57.609 --> 00:31:58.450
[S10] owns it.

492
00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:03.970
[S10] Radical transparency is a very effective tool in terms of building trust and putting

493
00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:05.029
[S10] things to light.

494
00:32:05.059 --> 00:32:10.710
[S10] Believe me, none of this is perfect, but it's certainly been miraculous for us.

495
00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:12.990
[S11] Excellent.

496
00:32:13.019 --> 00:32:17.599
[S11] Why do you think organizations aren't going towards radical transparency?

497
00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:22.190
[S10] First of all, I think that they inevitably will.

498
00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:28.349
[S10] I mean, you must know that it's so easy to collect data on everybody.

499
00:32:33.099 --> 00:32:35.599
[S10] So, everybody's gonna know what everybody's like.

500
00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:38.480
[S10] And then the question is, who's got the radical transparency?

501
00:32:42.049 --> 00:32:45.690
[S10] And then what are you gonna do with the radical transparency?

502
00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:49.140
[S10] You're gonna make what you're doing with it radically transparent?

503
00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:49.690
[S10] Okay?

504
00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:57.309
[S10] So, I think evolutionarily, we're going to go to this, you know, radical truthfulness

505
00:32:57.339 --> 00:33:01.200
[S10] and radical transparency, because it's gonna be tough to hide.

506
00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:07.880
[S10] I think it's a control thing and a time thing.

507
00:33:07.910 --> 00:33:12.559
[S10] So, let's say a control thing means you're a decision maker and all these people have

508
00:33:12.589 --> 00:33:14.559
[S10] all these different points of view.

509
00:33:14.589 --> 00:33:18.950
[S10] And how meritocratic is it to hear all this and will never resolve those things?

510
00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:21.750
[S10] Somebody just wants to sort of get on with their decision-making.

511
00:33:21.779 --> 00:33:26.559
[S10] And I think that that's because they're still sort of in this, what I believe will be

512
00:33:26.589 --> 00:33:35.589
[S10] an old-world mindset, that it is all in the head, rather than out of the head and

513
00:33:35.619 --> 00:33:40.089
[S10] in that notion of how do you make the best algorithmic decision-making so it's not in

514
00:33:40.119 --> 00:33:40.950
[S10] the head?

515
00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:46.089
[S10] But we still have the, it's in my head and I wanna do what I wanna do and I know better

516
00:33:46.119 --> 00:33:47.089
[S10] and how do I do that?

517
00:33:47.119 --> 00:33:48.650
[S10] That's a big part of it.

518
00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:52.500
[S10] And then the thinking, of course, about the time of how do you resolve it with all

519
00:33:52.529 --> 00:33:53.420
[S10] these people?

520
00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:58.400
[S10] So that you have a real time-efficient, effective way of having an idea meritocracy.

521
00:33:58.430 --> 00:34:00.140
[S10] Those are the barriers.

522
00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:02.809
[S10] And then there's this to you thing, okay?

523
00:34:02.839 --> 00:34:05.089
[S10] That emotional you thing.

524
00:34:05.119 --> 00:34:07.400
[S10] Those are the, I would say, the main barriers.

525
00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:10.619
[S11] Now, you said what's important for the mission of Bridgewater is having meaningful

526
00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:12.650
[S11] relationships.

527
00:34:12.679 --> 00:34:18.119
[S11] Have you found that with this transparency, you've been able to just, I guess, supercharge

528
00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:20.340
[S11] the relationships that you've had in the company?

529
00:34:22.190 --> 00:34:27.550
[S10] Yeah, I'd just say, we don't even have, yes, but we don't even have to go to the

530
00:34:27.579 --> 00:34:28.909
[S10] company level.

531
00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:34.280
[S10] I'm just saying, any group of individuals can decide whether they're going to be radically

532
00:34:34.300 --> 00:34:36.860
[S10] truthful and transparent and on the same mission.

533
00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:41.960
[S10] You get five people together and they say, we're gonna be on that.

534
00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:45.780
[S10] And that's largely where it started, too, because I start the company and I say, how

535
00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:47.489
[S10] am I gonna be with each other?

536
00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:54.460
[S10] So, it has, because that honesty and being on that mission is gonna bring people closer

537
00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:54.880
[S10] together.

538
00:34:54.909 --> 00:34:56.590
[S10] It almost brings everybody closer together.

539
00:34:56.610 --> 00:35:00.579
[S10] The hiding stuff and the absence of trust is bad.

540
00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:05.110
[S10] And not only the absence of trust, but the, you can't have real ownership.

541
00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:12.110
[S12] If you don't understand and don't have a say, you know, everybody and like I'm saying,

542
00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:16.869
[S12] everybody has the right and obligation to make sense of things.

543
00:35:16.869 --> 00:35:18.780
[S12] It's powerful.

544
00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:24.929
[S12] And when you're in it together, something much more magical happens than a paycheck

545
00:35:24.929 --> 00:35:26.679
[S12] and a job, you know?

546
00:35:26.679 --> 00:35:32.250
[S13] Now, when you've created your principles, has there been processes where you said,

547
00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:35.969
[S13] maybe this principle worked at this point, but now we need to reevaluate it?

548
00:35:35.969 --> 00:35:37.679
[S12] Constantly.

549
00:35:37.679 --> 00:35:42.570
[S12] Because, like you, so, I guess I wanted to describe it.

550
00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:50.929
[S12] I think everything happens, just about everything, happens over and over again.

551
00:35:50.929 --> 00:35:54.869
[S12] We instead look at things as individual things that are coming up.

552
00:35:54.869 --> 00:35:57.139
[S12] So it's like we're in a snowstorm.

553
00:35:57.139 --> 00:36:03.369
[S12] But if you just sort of say, what species of thing, what species of encounter am I

554
00:36:03.369 --> 00:36:04.480
[S12] having?

555
00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:10.000
[S12] And you write a principle for it, and you write that, then when the next one comes along

556
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:16.250
[S12] again, you see, ah, it's that species, and you go to that principle and you look at it,

557
00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.719
[S12] and everyone's slightly different.

558
00:36:18.719 --> 00:36:23.949
[S12] And that leads to then the refinement of the principles and those differences, right?

559
00:36:23.949 --> 00:36:26.809
[S12] And then to do that transparently is very powerful.

560
00:36:26.809 --> 00:36:30.170
[S12] So, yeah, always.

561
00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:35.570
[S12] And you realize, of course, that no principle is perfect.

562
00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:36.670
[S12] No principle works.

563
00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:41.599
[S12] So those different gradations get more fleshed out, become clearer.

564
00:36:41.599 --> 00:36:44.469
[S12] And that's what the, that's what the Book of Principles is about.

565
00:36:44.469 --> 00:36:48.530
[S12] It's a, it's a bunch of these things, and but you'll see how they've sort of evolved

566
00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:51.469
[S12] from this encounter and that encounter, and you get refinement.

567
00:36:51.469 --> 00:36:54.469
[S12] And I would urge you individually to do this.

568
00:36:54.469 --> 00:36:58.929
[S12] It's one of the most wonderful things that's happened to me, you know?

569
00:36:58.929 --> 00:37:03.710
[S12] When something comes along, some situation, particularly a painful situation, therefore

570
00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:07.500
[S12] might signal, you don't want another one of those.

571
00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:16.170
[S12] After you get past your pain, or at the end of it, record your pain, and then think about

572
00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:18.269
[S12] principles and write them down.

573
00:37:18.269 --> 00:37:21.579
[S12] How would you handle that type of situation?

574
00:37:21.579 --> 00:37:23.639
[S12] Write it down like a diary.

575
00:37:23.639 --> 00:37:28.980
[S12] And when you accumulate those and share those, that's very powerful.

576
00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:35.480
[S12] I'm going to create an app in which I'm, not only include, we have an app which we

577
00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:41.699
[S12] call Coach, which, somebody says, I'm in this situation, what should I do?

578
00:37:41.699 --> 00:37:46.769
[S12] And then they go to that Coach and there's, and the relevant principles come up.

579
00:37:46.769 --> 00:37:52.650
[S12] But rather than just my principles, I'm going to make it that each successful person, I'm

